{
  "dom_id": "step-q214099",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The lines $8x-2y=-2$ and $kx-5y=15$ are parallel. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $8x-2y=-2,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-2y & =-2 \\\\ -2y & =-8x-2 \\\\ 2y & =8x+2 \\\\ y & =4x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $4.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-5y=15,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-5y & =15 \\\\ -5y & =-kx+15 \\\\ 5y & =kx-15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{5}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{k}{5}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $\\frac{k}{5}=4.$ We solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{5} & =4 \\\\ k & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
